WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:05.900
Every boat is held together, tied up and anchored with rope, and on a boat we call it line.

2
00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:10.550
Using the wrong line for the job is a quiet way to lose a boat.

3
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.050
So start with what it’s made of.

4
00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:15.950
Nylon is strong, and it stretches.

5
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:23.025
That stretch soaks up shock loads, which makes nylon the choice for anchor rodes, dock lines and towlines.

6
00:00:23.275 --> 00:00:24.925
It doesn’t float.

7
00:00:25.175 --> 00:00:30.150
Polyester, often sold as Dacron, is strong with very little stretch.

8
00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:33.850
It’s for halyards and sheets, where you don’t want any give.

9
00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:36.100
Polypropylene floats.

10
00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:39.225
That’s why it’s on ring buoys and ski ropes.

11
00:00:39.475 --> 00:00:42.400
But it’s weaker, and the sun breaks it down.

12
00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:49.075
And Spectra and Dyneema are the modern ones: extremely strong, very low stretch, and they float.

13
00:00:49.325 --> 00:00:51.625
So which one goes on your anchor?

14
00:00:52.625 --> 00:00:54.125
Nylon.

15
00:00:54.375 --> 00:00:57.425
You want that spring in the line when the boat surges.
